Q: Expo-EAS build for android APK A: <pre><code>import { View, Text, TouchableOpacity, StyleSheet } from &quot;react-native&quot;;
import { WebView } from &quot;react-native-webview&quot;;
import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;

export default function App() {
  const [url, setUrl] = useState(null);

  const links = [
    { name: &quot;STM&quot;, url: &quot;https://www.stm.jus.br&quot; },
    { name: &quot;TRF2&quot;, url: &quot;https://www.trf2.jus.br&quot; },
    { name: &quot;JFRJ&quot;, url: &quot;https://www.jfrj.jus.br&quot; },
    { name: &quot;JFES&quot;, url: &quot;https://www.jfes.jus.br&quot; },
    { name: &quot;TSE&quot;, url: &quot;http://www.tse.jus.br&quot; },
    { name: &quot;Detran RJ&quot;, url: &quot;https://certidaocacciifppcerj.detran.rj.gov.br/&quot; },
  ];

  if (url) {
    return &lt;WebView source={{ uri: url }} style={{ flex: 1 }} /&gt;;
  }

  return (
    &lt;View style={styles.container}&gt;
      &lt;Text style={styles.title}&gt;VIG Justiça&lt;/Text&gt;

      {links.map((link, index) =&gt; (
        &lt;TouchableOpacity
          key={index}
          style={styles.button}
          onPress={() =&gt; setUrl(link.url)}
        &gt;
          &lt;Text style={styles.text}&gt;{link.name}&lt;/Text&gt;
        &lt;/TouchableOpacity&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/View&gt;
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    justifyContent: &quot;center&quot;,
    padding: 20,
    backgroundColor: &quot;#0f172a&quot;,
  },
  title: {
    fontSize: 26,
    fontWeight: &quot;bold&quot;,
    marginBottom: 30,
    textAlign: &quot;center&quot;,
    color: &quot;#fff&quot;,
  },
  button: {
    backgroundColor: &quot;#1e293b&quot;,
    padding: 18,
    marginBottom: 12,
    borderRadius: 14,
    borderWidth: 1,
    borderColor: &quot;#334155&quot;,
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 18,
    textAlign: &quot;center&quot;,
    color: &quot;#fff&quot;,
  },
});
</code></pre>

Q: Get names of the params passed to a C# method A: <p>(Might solve your problem but mayu create a performance problem)<br />
I believe what you pretend to do would be solved by the folloowing code:</p>
<p>instead of receiveing a (params Object[] args )<br />
receive a Object and read it like this:</p>
<pre><code>void MyMethod(string something, object param)
{
    if (param == null) return;

    var properties = param.GetType().GetProperties();

    foreach (var property in properties)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(property.Name);
        Console.WriteLine(property.GetType().Name);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And to call it, you could use this:</p>
<pre><code>var arg0 = &quot;banana&quot;;
var arg1 = 123;

MyMethod(&quot;something&quot;, new { arg0, arg1 });
</code></pre>

Q: During template argument deduction and substitution of function template, do class template specialization get implicily instantiated? A: <p>Fixing your example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>extern boo&lt;foo&lt;int&gt;&gt; b;  // Does not instantiate boo&lt;foo&lt;int&gt;&gt;
(someFunc)(b);
</code></pre>
<p>A template class is instantiated whenever the instantiated class needs to be a complete type.</p>
<p>In your example with <code>someFunc(b)</code>, the type needs to be completed to perform ADL. The brackets prevent that.</p>
<p>During overload resolution, template argument deduction is done to check if the first template can match. This is purely at the type level and does not yet need the type to be complete.</p>
<p>For the first overload, after <code>T = foo&lt;int&gt;</code> is deduced, the template specification's declaration is synthesized (<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/temp.over#1.1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">[temp.over]p1</a>). A function's arguments are required to have a complete type, so this is where the class would be instantiated. Similarly for your second overload, <code>typename T::boo_type</code> requires the type to be complete.</p>
<p>So this is closest to your (2), the template is instantiated after the types are substituted in if necessary.</p>
<p>You might want to think about this example instead: <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/bbcTj4Ped" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/bbcTj4Ped</a></p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct foo {
    static_assert(false);
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
void someFunc(foo&lt;T&gt;&amp;);  // #1

template&lt;typename T&gt;
typename T::type someFunc(T&amp;);  // #2

void f() {
    extern foo&lt;int&gt; b;
    (someFunc)(b);
}
</code></pre>
<p>When the second overload is present, this fails to compile because it completes <code>foo&lt;int&gt;</code> during overload resolution (after <code>T = foo&lt;int&gt;</code> is substituted in, even though it wouldn't eventually be chosen). But if you comment out the second overload, then it does compile.</p>
